comments.message:
nittest of nits:
I tend to lean towards early exit rather than if/else, even if it means calling std::apply in more than one spot. I also I know this is an unpopular opinion, but goto isn't unreasonable for this. maybe I'll lose some credibility for that but for making sure all error paths run a function, its a decent tool when used sparingly. you can use RAII-semantics if you'd like which I don't personally love for this, just because its non-obvious

```
if (ec)
{
    std::get<0>(responseData) = ec;
    std::apply(handler, responseData);
    return;
}

// your non ec code here
```

or
```
if (ec)
{
    std::get<0>(responseData) = ec;
    goto apply;
}

// your non ec code here
apply:
    std::apply(handler, responseData);
}
```

or (I don't much care for this one, Id rather just call std::apply multiple times)
```
template<typename F>
struct RunOnExit {
    RunOnExit(F&& f) : f{f} {}
    ~RunOnExit() { f(); }
    F f;
};

RunOnExit exitHandler([&responseData, &handler]() { std::apply(handler, responseData); });

if (ec)
{
   std::get<0>(responseData) = ec;
   return;
}

// happy-ish path
}
```
